---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ExternalLink from "@/components/ExternalLink.astro";
import ReportIssue from "@/components/ReportIssue.astro";
import { SITE_URL } from "@/config/site";
import { breadcrumbWithHome } from "@/lib/jsonld";
import { ROUTES, absoluteRoute } from "@/lib/routes";
const pageUrl = absoluteRoute(SITE_URL, ROUTES.methodology);
const methodologyJsonLd = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    headline: "Methodology",
    description:
      "How projectcert collects, normalizes, and verifies state EL teacher certification data — including the 2019 Leider et al. baseline and the 2026 per-state SEA re-verification workflow.",
    url: pageUrl,
    inLanguage: "en-US",
    publisher: { "@id": `${SITE_URL}/#organization` },
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
    citation: "https://doi.org/10.14507/epaa.29.5279",
  },
  breadcrumbWithHome([{ name: "Methodology", url: pageUrl }]),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="How projectcert collects, normalizes, and verifies state EL teacher certification data."
  type="article"
  jsonLd={methodologyJsonLd}
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-8 prose-style">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-ink">Methodology</h1>

    <h2 class="mt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Seed data</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink-muted">
      The 51-state baseline comes from Leider, Colombo &amp; Nerlino
      (2021), <em>Education Policy Analysis Archives</em> 29(100). The
      authors conducted a document analysis of every U.S. state education
      agency's (SEA) public credentialing documents between October and
      December 2019, plus each SEA's professional teaching standards.
      <ExternalLink class="text-accent hover:underline" href="https://doi.org/10.14507/epaa.29.5279">Read the paper</ExternalLink>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Use of AI in data collection and analysis</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink-muted">
      Beyond the 2019 baseline, the catalog has been re-verified,
      extended, and prepared for publication using an AI agent (Claude,
      Anthropic) under direct human supervision. The agent retrieved
      current SEA documents, extracted the relevant credentialing rules,
      drafted the per-state coding against the same coding scheme used
      for the 2019 baseline, traced pre-2019 statutory and case-law
      history, and produced the prose surfaces that wrap the underlying
      data. Every field continues to trace to a cited source URL with a
      retrieval date, and dated source snapshots are archived per state
      in the project repository for independent inspection. The 2019
      baseline coding from Leider, Colombo &amp; Nerlino (2021) was not
      generated by the agent; it is the authors' published document
      analysis, retained verbatim and used as the comparison point
      against which every subsequent re-verification is recorded.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-muted">
      AI-assisted extraction is fallible. The agent may misread an SEA
      regulation, conflate adjacent endorsements, miss a recent rule
      revision, or apply the wrong canonical category. Readers are
      asked to treat any field outside the original 2019 collection as
      provisional until cross-checked against the cited source URL,
      and to verify load-bearing claims directly with the SEA before
      relying on them. Corrections — including disagreements with the
      coding, missing history events, and broken or stale citations —
      are welcome via the form below.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Data captured</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink-muted">Each state record encodes, per the <a class="text-accent hover:underline" href={ROUTES.glossary}>canonical taxonomy</a>:</p>
    <ul class="mt-2 list-disc pl-6 text-ink-muted space-y-1">
      <li>The percentage of public-school students classified as ELs.</li>
      <li>Whether a bilingual education credential is offered, and (if so) whether it is available as a standalone certification, an add-on endorsement, or both.</li>
      <li>Whether an ELD credential is offered, and the same standalone/add-on breakdown.</li>
      <li>Requirements to earn each credential: completion of an approved program, coursework, practicum, content test, and (for bilingual) demonstrated proficiency in a non-English language.</li>
      <li>Whether SEI training is mandated for all teachers.</li>
      <li>Whether the SEA's professional teaching standards explicitly reference diverse / cultural / linguistic / EL students.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Provenance</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink-muted">
      Every fact-bearing field on every state record cites at least
      one source — each with a URL, a retrieval date, and an
      attribution to either the Leider et al. 2021 collection or the
      2026 re-verification pass. A state record with no cited source
      cannot be published; the build refuses it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Verification — current status</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink-muted">
      The 2019 baseline data is treated as a <em>seed</em>, not the
      published version. Before public launch, every state must be
      re-verified against the SEA's <em>current</em> public documents
      and move from its 2019 baseline coding to the 2026 verified
      coding.
      <a class="text-accent hover:underline" href={ROUTES.verification}>Track the per-state progress</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-6 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Reporting outdated or incorrect data</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink-muted">
      State credentialing requirements evolve regularly, and AI-assisted
      coding can introduce its own mistakes. Practitioners, SEA staff,
      and researchers who identify outdated, miscoded, or insufficiently
      cited entries are encouraged to flag them — field-level
      corrections from people who work with these credentials directly
      are preferred to reliance on the project's re-verification
      schedule alone.
    </p>

    <ReportIssue variant="inline" pageType="methodology" />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
